--TEST--
Core\Csrf and Core\SignedCookie share one construction and one key type, and neither one's output is the other's input — the domain tag in a token's plaintext is what refuses the confusion — ADR 0060 §§ 1, 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Edges. The boundary this case sits on is the one a deployment reaches by
// itself: an application generates one key with `Core\Crypto::generateKey()`
// and uses it for its signed cookies and its CSRF tokens, because nothing
// stops it. Without a domain tag in the plaintext, a signed cookie carrying a
// session identifier *is* a valid CSRF token for that session — authentic
// under the same key, over the same construction, with the same bytes.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/csrf.rs` owns the tag and its version.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];
string $session = "sid-ada";

// A signed cookie of exactly the session identifier, which is the value an
// application most plausibly seals: it is what a session cookie holds.
string $cookie = Core\SignedCookie::seal($session, $ring);
if (Core\Csrf::verify($cookie, $session, $key) == false) {
    echo "a signed cookie is not a token", "\n";
} else {
    echo "a signed cookie passed as a token", "\n";
}

// And the reverse: a token opens as a cookie, because it is the same
// construction under the same key and authenticity is not the thing that
// differs — but what comes out is not the session identifier, so a program
// reading a token as a session cookie gets a value that matches no session.
// That asymmetry is the point: the tag does not make the two unforgeable
// against each other, it makes them *non-interchangeable*.
string $token = Core\Csrf::issue($session, $key);
if (Core\SignedCookie::open($token, $ring) == $session) {
    echo "a token opened as the bare session", "\n";
} else {
    echo "a token is not a session cookie", "\n";
}

// The tag is a prefix of the plaintext, not a suffix or a separator, so what a
// cookie would have to carry to be mistaken for a token is longer than the
// session identifier by a fixed amount. A member that appended the tag instead
// would pass every line above and collide on a session identifier that
// happened to end in one.
if (Core\Str::length(Core\SignedCookie::open($token, $ring)) == Core\Str::length($session) + 12) {
    echo "the tag is 12 characters of prefix", "\n";
} else {
    echo "the plaintext is a different shape", "\n";
}

// A token is still a token afterwards — nothing above consumed it — and it is
// still bound to its own session and no other.
if (Core\Csrf::verify($token, $session, $key)) {
    echo "and the token still verifies", "\n";
} else {
    echo "the token stopped verifying", "\n";
}
if (Core\Csrf::verify($token, "sid-bob", $key) == false) {
    echo "against its own session only", "\n";
} else {
    echo "it verified against another session", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
a signed cookie is not a token
a token is not a session cookie
the tag is 12 characters of prefix
and the token still verifies
against its own session only
